Title: "Variadic Slices"
Id: "2e12b00b-2bb1-4b07-9e5d-b0361bb9e707"

Text: "A slice selects the elements of a variadic command parameter. The supported
one-dimensional forms are:

```mlg-fragment
x...
x[0...n]
x[1...n]
x[0...i_...n]
x[1...i_...n]
```

Individual elements are referenced with ordinary subset syntax such as `x[0]`,
`x[1]`, or `x[i_]`.

A slice **broadcasts** when it is the left operand of `:=`, `=`, `!=`, `is`,
`is?`, or a quoted specification operator, including that operator's predicate
form. A slice may also appear on the right of `:=`, `=`, or `!=`; paired slices
must spell exactly the same start, binder, and end.

In a clause list, the broadcast produces one clause per element, so
`. x[1...n] = 0` inside `anyOf:` means one alternative for every indexed `x`.

A two-dimensional slice gives each axis independently as a single index, a
range, or `...` for the whole axis:

```mlg-fragment
x[a...i_...b, c...j_...d]
x[a...b, c...d]
x[a...b, ...]
x[..., c...d]
x[..., ...]
x[a, ...]
x[..., c]
```

At least one axis must be a range or `...`; `x[a, c]` is the ordinary single
element. A 2D slice broadcasts under the same relations as a 1D slice, so
`x[a...b, c...d] is \\real` applies to every selected `x[i,j]`. Paired 2D slices
must use exactly the same two axis selections.

Slices are accepted only by these broadcast operations and by the variadic
builtins; a slice is rejected as an ordinary operand.

```mlg-fragment
\\\\map{x[1...i_...n]}:to{x[i_] + 1}
\\\\map{x[1...i_...n], y[1...i_...n]}:to{x[i_] + y[i_] + 1}
\\\\leftReduce{`+`}:on{x[1...n]}
\\\\rightReduce{`+`}:on{x[1...i_...n]}
```

`\\\\map` maps aligned slices pointwise: all input slices must have exactly the
same range spelling, and multiple slices are zipped rather than combined as a
Cartesian product. The result expression may use the bound index.

The reduction builtins take a binary operator and one slice, select left and
right association respectively, and render with an ellipsis such as
`x_1 + ... + x_n`. A bound slice also displays its middle term, as in
`x_1 + ... + x_i + ... + x_n`.

Equality chains such as `x = y = z` are ordinary left-associated equality
expressions and are unrelated to slices."
Id: "6d948156-7908-496a-830f-5f1ed9cdd5a1"
